  "suspect_nets": ["and_cross0", "and_cross1", "and_same0", "and_same1"],
  "algebraic_masking_correct": false,
  "vulnerability_summary": "The masked_and module contains a fundamental algebraic masking defect: the Boolean masking formula used for q0 and q1 does not correctly reconstruct to (a0 XOR a1) AND (b0 XOR b1) under all input combinations. The root cause is a mathematical error in how the partial products and_same0 (a0&b0), and_same1 (a1&b1), and_cross0 (a0&b1), and_cross1 (a1&b0) are combined with the mask bit r -- the XOR recombination formula itself is algebraically incorrect, meaning the masking scheme fails even at steady state with no timing or glitch effects involved. This is a scheme-level correctness bug in the masking construction, not a side-channel or timing-dependent issue; the module would leak the secret even in an idealized zero-delay simulation because the shares do not cancel out to the correct AND result. Fixing this requires redesigning the masking algebra itself rather than any register retiming.",
  "hardening_proposal": {
    "registers_to_add": ["a0", "a1", "b0", "b1", "r"],
    "placement_description": "Insert registers on a0, a1, b0, b1, and r at the module's input boundary, clocking them into flip-flops before they feed the and_same0, and_same1, and_cross0, and_cross1 partial-product gates and the subsequent q0/q1 XOR combination stage, in addition to correcting the underlying algebraic masking formula so that q0 XOR q1 actually equals the correct AND result at steady state."
  },
  "confidence": 0.9
}

{
  "suspect_nets": ["and_cross0", "and_same0", "and_same1"],
  "algebraic_masking_correct": true,
  "vulnerability_summary": "The masked_and module is algebraically correct: q0 XOR q1 equals (a0 XOR a1) AND (b0 XOR b1) for every input combination in steady state, confirmed across all vectors in inputs/sim_trace.vcd.txt. The masking algebra itself is NOT flawed. The leakage instead arises from glitch propagation through unregistered combinational recombination logic. The module has no pipeline registers between the input ports (a0,a1,b0,b1,r) and the output ports (q0,q1): and_same0 (a0&b0), and_same1 (a1&b1), and_cross0 (a0&b1) are all computed combinationally and merged in the same clock cycle at the q1 combination stage (and q0 = and_same0 ^ r). Because a0, a1, b0, and b1 do not arrive at these gates with identical delay in real silicon (differing fan-in depths and wire/gate delays), and_cross0 experiences transient glitches during which signals derived from both shares of the same operand are simultaneously influencing switching activity on the same node before it settles. This transient joint dependency on both shares of an operand within a single clock cycle defeats the first-order independence (non-completeness) property that the masking scheme relies on, even though the final settled value of q0/q1 is correctly masked. An attacker with power/EM measurement capability can perform univariate CPA/DPA on the transient activity at and_cross0 or the q1 combination node to recover a first-order-exploitable correlation with the unmasked secret, despite the masking being algebraically sound at steady state.",
  "hardening_proposal": {
    "registers_to_add": ["a0", "a1", "b0", "b1", "r"],
    "placement_description": "Insert registers on a0, a1, b0, b1, and r at the module's input boundary, clocking them into flip-flops before they feed the and_same0, and_same1, and_cross0 partial-product gates and the subsequent q0/q1 XOR combination stage. Equivalently, registers could instead be placed on and_same0, and_same1, and_cross0, and r immediately after the partial-product AND gates but strictly before the final XOR tree that produces q0 and q1. Either placement ensures that within a single clock cycle no combinational path carries a glitch derived from both shares of the same operand into a shared node; each share pair is latched and stabilized before recombination, restoring cycle-by-cycle non-completeness/glitch-robustness. Registering only the final q0/q1 outputs would be insufficient, since the glitch-vulnerable recombination at and_cross0 or the q1 XOR node would still occur upstream of such a register within the same cycle."
  },
  "confidence": 0.95
}

